{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfv6LLCqqMxeECvuTrsTY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both findings are ruled FIX, and DB's defect is one I created in sittings 005 and 006. The verdict is committed as `92bb3de96` with `WI: WI-887`, in `docs/reviews/wi-887/008-ADJUDICATE-5dc2d41.md`. I edited no code, registry or review.\n\n**DA: FIX.** I reproduced it. A review record whose `rollout_requests` is the string `\"oops\"` passes the store's reader as a valid record. The compaction bookkeeping then crashes with a `TypeError`, and nothing catches it before the review call itself fails.\n- **Why it's in scope:** a malformed file in the kit's own store isn't the compromised-host case that PROCESS.md §6 excludes. This lane's own approved text (its Trust ruling, SR-239 and LLR-270) promises that a malformed record is treated as no record, so the code breaks a behaviour the lane declared supported.\n- **Remedy:** this is the third round of the same problem, each round finding one more field. So rather than typing `rollout_requests` alone, the fix declares a type for every optional field any consumer reads: the compaction fields and the occupancy and use fields. Add a parametrised regression with one mistyped case per field.\n- **Part I rejected:** the reviewer's ask to also check values inside the lease. Every consumer of the lease already checks its types.\n\n**DB: FIX, but narrower than the reviewer asked.** I didn't uphold the six-way split; SR-239 is still one decision about reviewer-session retention. The real defect is mechanical:\n- With SR-239 approved, `trace.py --strict` exits 1 because the row has two `shall` sentences, and `check.py` runs that check.\n- The check skips `Drafted` rows, so it only fired after my sitting-006 approval.\n- The second `shall` comes from my sitting-005 instruction to write the fail-closed rule as an \"If … then\" clause.\n- A recorded waiver wouldn't clear it, because the check doesn't read one.\n\n**Remedy:** get back to one `shall` per row. Either move the fail-closed rule into a sibling \"If … then\" SR, or fold it into SR-239's single `shall` as part of the response. The changed rows then need another adjudication sitting.\n\nTwo more things the verdict notes, outside both findings:\n- `trace.py --strict` also fails on LLR-292's word \"minimal\". That predates this lane and is unchanged in it.\n- There's a process gap: the closure check before a sitting should run this form check on `Drafted` rows, so a failure like DB's shows up before approval rather than after.\n\nThe `.requested` binding file is still untracked, left for the coordinator."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":352,"cache_read_input_tokens":454685,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":352},"output_tokens":728,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d52360a6-7377-4d1e-a658-4cbc4052baa8","uuid":"2ad1ea18-f223-46cf-8638-2b8e02f25021","timestamp":"2026-10-11T07:56:59.944Z","request_id":"req_011Cfv6LKipiTmpDbP1jMq1J"}
